WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:03.840
Hi! This is a BBC Learning English series

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:08.360
all about something that everyone loves: phrasal verbs.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:10.200
I'm Georgie, and in this video,

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.200
we're learning phrasal verbs that you can use to talk about friendships

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.960
or relationships between people.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:19.040
Let's get into it.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.160
If you 'get on' with someone,

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:28.680
you like them and have a friendly relationship with them.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.160
If you don't get on...

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:33.120
well, it's the opposite.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:35.560
We get on so well!

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.080
It's so good to have friendships

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.080
at work. Isn't it!

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:40.800
If you 'fall out' with someone,

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.680
it means you have an argument and

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:45.360
you stop being friendly with them.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.920
The way you make your coffee is terrible.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:52.480
Stop being rude or we're going to fall out!

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:54.760
If you 'run into' someone,

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:58.880
It means that you meet them unexpectedly. We can also say

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:03.880
'bump into' someone. Neil!

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:06.840
I wasn't expecting to run into you at the office today.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.920
I thought you'd be at home.
Well...

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.120
If you 'hear from' someone,

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:17.560
it means you receive communication from someone, usually via phone call, email

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:19.280
or text message.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.560
I asked if you were coming in today, but I didn't hear from you.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:23.600
We fell out, Neil.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.000
I thought you were still angry.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.840
If you 'make up' with someone,

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.840
It means you become friends again
after falling out or having an argument.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:34.880
Well, I'm sorry. I didn't mean to

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:36.680
insult your coffee-making style.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:38.600
That's OK. I forgive you.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:41.760
Yay! I'm glad we made up.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:43.600
If you 'hang out' with someone,

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.920
it means you spend time relaxing or socialising with them.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:50.560
It's so nice that we can hang out like this again

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.160
now that we've made up.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:53.160
I agree.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.640
And finally, if you 'catch up' with someone,

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.320
it means you talk to them about what's been happening in your lives

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:00.520
since the last time

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.600
you spoke or hung out.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.840
Neil, it feels like ages since I've seen you.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:06.200
We really need to catch up!

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:09.560
I know! What's been going on in your life lately?

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:12.400
Remember, don't freak out! Just come back to

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:17.600
BBC Learning English, and we'll work on more phrasal verbs and how to use them.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:19.440
See you next time!

